<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!-- launchd SYSTEM DAEMON template, installed to
     /Library/LaunchDaemons/com.pearcal.seeder.plist by the .pkg post-install
     script. __LOG_PATH__, __USER__, __GROUP__, __USER_HOME__ and __DATA_DIR__ are
     filled in by postinstall; __VERSION__ at build time.

     A SYSTEM DAEMON, NOT A LAUNCHAGENT, and that is the whole point of this file.

     This used to be a LaunchAgent in ~/Library/LaunchAgents, and the comment here
     claimed it "survives logout via KeepAlive". That was false. An agent there
     loads into gui/501, a domain whose header reads `type = login`,
     `creator = loginwindow` - loginwindow builds it at login and tears it down at
     logout. KeepAlive restarts a job that EXITS; it does not exempt one from its
     domain's teardown. Measured on the mac-mini 2026-07-31 with a heartbeat agent
     across one real logout/login: last beat `21:58:04Z pid=12235`, then 48s of
     nothing, then `21:58:52Z pid=13652`. Killed at logout; a DIFFERENT process
     started at login. A crash-plus-KeepAlive would have resumed in seconds and
     would not have waited for the login. A seeder that stops seeding at logout is
     the one failure this program exists to avoid.

     macOS also refuses to bootstrap an arbitrary job into user/501 (error 5), so
     it is the GUI session or the system domain, with nothing in between.

     RUN AS THE USER, NOT AS ROOT - while still being a system-domain daemon.
     Those are independent: what survives logout is living in the system domain
     rather than gui/501, and UserName only decides which credentials the job runs
     with. That keeps the seed store at ~/.pearcal-seed exactly where it is, owned
     by the same account. Running as root instead would leave root-owned files in
     the user's own store; PearTune hit precisely that on 2026-08-08 and had to
     back it out, because it left the unprivileged app unable to write files it
     supposedly owned.

     Same pattern, already proven on this hardware: PearTune's
     com.peerloom.peartune daemon, which survived a real reboot with nobody
     logged in. -->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.pearcal.seeder</string>

  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/usr/local/lib/pearcal-seeder/pearcal-seeder</string>
  </array>

  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>SEEDER_PORT</key><string>8731</string>
    <key>PEARCAL_SEEDER_VERSION</key><string>__VERSION__</string>

    <!-- THE SEED STORE IS THE SEEDER'S IDENTITY, so it is set EXPLICITLY rather
         than derived. The installed launcher is a wrapper that resolves its data
         dir as ${PEARCAL_SEED_DATA:-$HOME/.pearcal-seed}, and launchd does not
         give a system daemon the same HOME an agent inherits from the login
         session. Left to chance the daemon would come up on a different, empty
         store and this seeder would look brand new to every peer that knows it.
         HOME is set too, for anything else that reads the home directory. -->
    <key>PEARCAL_SEED_DATA</key><string>__DATA_DIR__</string>
    <key>HOME</key><string>__USER_HOME__</string>
  </dict>

  <!-- See the UserName note above: system domain for the lifetime, user
       credentials so the seed store and its ownership are unchanged. -->
  <key>UserName</key>
  <string>__USER__</string>
  <key>GroupName</key>
  <string>__GROUP__</string>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>

  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <true/>

  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>__LOG_PATH__</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>__LOG_PATH__</string>

  <key>ProcessType</key>
  <string>Background</string>
</dict>
</plist>
